# Evaporated Undoped Spiro‐OMeTAD Enables Stable Perovskite Solar Cells Exceeding 20% Efficiency

## abstract
Thermal evaporation (TE) as a scalable and low‐cost technique for fabrication of organic hole transport materials (HTMs) typically produces low photovoltaic performance and poor device reproducibility in the application of perovskite solar cells (PSCs), and there is a clear need to understand the weaknesses of TE. Here, a versatile manufacturing technology, solvent‐annealing assisted thermal evaporation (SATE), enabling effective modulation of organic film morphology as well as optoelectronic properties, is introduced. The SATE method produces undoped spiro‐OMeTAD layers with high density, good film homogeneity, enhanced conductivity, and remarkable film stability, all of which are superior to that made by conventional TE. In addition, SATE films eliminate the dopant induced degradation mechanism and simultaneously improve the electrical conductivity of undoped HTMs. Significantly, the resulting devices yield a 36% enhancement of power conversion efficiency (PCE) from 14.68% (TE) to 20.02% (SATE), which is the highest reported PCE for evaporated HTMs in n–i–p PSCs. Moreover, unencapsulated PSC devices with SATE demonstrate an impressive environmental and thermal stability by maintaining 85% of initial performance after 2500 h in air with 30% humidity. The high efficiency with simultaneously improved stability demonstrates SATE can be generally applicable to controllable fabrication of organic thin film and reliable devices.

Thermal evaporation (TE) as a scalable and low-cost technique for fabrication of organic hole transport materials (HTMs) typically produces low photovoltaic performance and poor device reproducibility in the application of perovskite solar cells (PSCs), and there is a clear need to understand the weaknesses of TE. Here, a versatile manufacturing technology, solvent-annealing assisted thermal evaporation (SATE), enabling effective modulation of organic film morphology as well as optoelectronic properties, is introduced. The SATE method produces undoped spiro-OMeTAD layers with high density, good film homogeneity, enhanced conductivity, and remarkable film stability, all of which are superior to that made by conventional TE. In addition, SATE films eliminate the dopant induced degradation mechanism and simultaneously improve the electrical conductivity of undoped HTMs. Significantly, the resulting devices yield a $36\%$ enhancement of power conversion efficiency (PCE) from $14.68\%$ (TE) to $20.02\%$ (SATE), which is the highest reported PCE for evaporated HTMs in n–i– $\cdot p$ PSCs. Moreover, unencapsulated PSC devices with SATE demonstrate an impressive environmental and thermal stability by maintaining $85\%$ of initial performance after $2500\text{\textperthousand}$ in air with $30\%$ humidity. The high efficiency with simultaneously improved stability demonstrates SATE can be generally applicable to controllable fabrication of organic thin film and reliable devices.
## 1. Introduction
Solution-processed perovskite semiconductors exhibit excellent optoelectronic properties and have been widely applied to a variety of device applications, particularly photovoltaic devices. Recent efforts on optimizing composition, device architecture and interfacial passivation, have enabled a power conversion efficiency (PCE) of over $25\%$ for perovskite solar cells (PSCs).[1–5] PSCs are thus recognized as one of the most promising candidates for next-generation photovoltaic (PV) technologies. Previous studies revealed that interfacial charge transport layers play pivotal roles in achieving high PCEs and good device stability.[5,6] Therein, hole transport materials (HTMs) represent a key component in n–i–p PSCs, which considerably affects hole collection efficiency and also protect the perovskite layer against degradation in harsh environments.[7] Spiro-OMeTAD $(2,2^{\prime},7,7^{\prime}$ -tetrakis $[N,N^{\prime}$ -di-p-methoxyphenylamine)- ${\mathfrak{g}},{\mathfrak{g}}^{\prime}$ - spirobifluorene) is the most used HTM in PSCs due to high film quality, mild processing conditions, appropriate bandgap $(2.98\ \mathrm{eV})$ and suitable energy levels.[8–10] In traditional spin coating (SP), a thick ( $250{\mathrm{-}}400\ \mathrm{nm})$ ) HTM layer is required for effectively isolating top metal electrode from the perovskite to ensure device longevity.[11] However, undoped HTMs with such high thicknesses exhibited low carrier mobility and poor conductivity, greatly limiting the charge collection efficiency and device PCEs. Researchers have pursued different doping strategies to increase the charge conductivity by introducing additives (e.g., lithium bis (trifluoromethylsulphonyl)imide (LiTFSI) and tert-butylpyridine (tBP)) in HTMs.[12–15] Several orders of magnitude higher conductivity $(>10^{-5}\ \mathrm{S}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ are achieved after doping compared to the pristine spiro-OMeTAD $(10^{-8}\ \mathrm{~S~}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ .[16–18] In traditional doped spiro-OMeTAD, the chemically sensitive components, such as hygroscopic LiTFSI and volatile tBP, tend to drive ion accumulation,[12,19] and perovskite corrosion,[20] greatly limiting device stability.[21–23] These deleterious effects associated with dopants significantly threaten the device reliability and their practical application. Although various types of representative dopant-free HTMs have been developed, such as tetrathiafulvalene derivative (TTF-1),[24] $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -spirobi[cyclopenta[2,1-b:3,4-b′] dithiophene] derivative (spiro-CPDT),[25] $N,N^{\prime}$ -bicarbazoles,[26] and D-A-Π-A-D structured DTB-FL,[27] most of these materials show relatively low PCEs $(<20\%)$ or tedious synthesis. Thereby, challenges still remain in developing dopant-free HTMs with high efficiency and stability.
To accomplish perovskite PV production, it is of vital importance to develop scalable film deposition methods for large modules. However, most HTM fabrication relies strongly on spin coating which is not practical for large areas. Additionally, the SP method is not environmentally friendly due to low production yield with high material waste. In this context, vacuum thermal evaporation (TE) has been briefly attempted to deposit HTMs for PSCs, including spiro-based small molecules,[28] copper phthalocyanine (CuPc),[29] tris[4-(5-phenylthiophene-2-yl) phenyl] amine (TPTPA)[30] and $\mathbf{\Psi}_{N,N^{\prime}}$ -Di(1-naphthyl)- $N,N^{\prime}$ -diphenyl-(1,10- biphenyl)-4,4′-diamine (NPB).[31] However, their PCEs are much lower than the solution-processed doped HTMs, and little is known about the factors limiting the photovoltaic performance of the TE-base HTMs. Therefore, there is a clear need to understand the weaknesses of TE, and to develop a versatile technology for manufacturing scalable and efficient HTM in PSCs.
Considering these challenges, we introduce a solventannealing assisted thermal evaporation (SATE) technique as a method for deposition of undoped spiro-OMeTAD thin film for efficient PSCs. Different from conventional TE, the SATE strategy can adjust molecular configuration, particularly a thickness reduction to form favorable molecular stacking with high density. In addition, the stronger molecular interaction within the dense film accelerates the hole hopping rate as well as enhances the charge collection efficiency. Moreover, the dense film made by SATE creates an effective barrier to hamper the water intake and metal diffusion. As a consequence, SATE method enables the formation of undoped spiro-OMeTAD thin films with high density, good film homogeneity, outstanding conductivity and remarkable stability. This work provides simple method to deposit conformal, dense, and homogeneous HTMs or other functional materials for efficient and stable optoelectronic devices.
## 2. Results and Discussion
Evaporation parameters substantially influence film growth and morphology in TE, and there are difficulties in tuning the molecular deposition in a vacuum environment (Figure 1A). Besides, it is challenging for undoped HTMs to balance high conductivity (low thickness) and stability with good barrier properties (high thickness and density). In order to achieve desired film morphology in a reproducible way, we developed SATE to fabricate undoped spiro-OMeTAD layers where the evaporated film is treated by solvent vapor and, followed by mild annealing (Figure  1A). SATE provides more flexibility to precise modulation of the film microstructure, morphology and quality, which are crucial to control the charge dynamics, barrier property (e.g. avoiding direct contact between Ag and perovskite) and device stability. In contrast to SP method, where a centripetal force and liquid surface tension produces a uniform and dense film, TE and SATE process induces high thermal energy into molecules and transforms vapor particles to solid film, facilitating a conformal coating. SATE is also a scalable method, and Figure 1B displays a SATE film deposited on a flexible substrate ( $\mathrm{5cm}\times5\mathrm{cm}\mathrm{]}$ .
Given that film thickness is a critical factor influencing HTM’s hole conductivity, we first studied the thickness effects $(10{-}130\ \mathrm{nm})$ ) on the photovoltaic performance of the n–i–p  devices with the structure of $\mathrm{ITO}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}/$ Perovskite/ HTM layer/Ag and TE-based spiro-OMeTAD. The color of TE films coated on perovskite became much darker as the thickness increased from 10 to $130\mathrm{nm}$ , which is consistent with the well-known thin-film interference phenomenon (Figure 1C).[32] In contrast to the uneven and inhomogeneous film from SP, TE enables smooth and featureless coatings across a range of thicknesses. Additionally, low light absorption from evaporated spiro-OMeTAD (Figure S1, Supporting Information) is an additional benefit for application in top-illuminated or tandem solar cells. For PSCs, a substantial PCE improvement from about $5\%$ to a maximum around $13\%$ was obtained as thickness increased from 10 to $70\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Figure  1C). The Ag film discoloration and corrosion were observed if the HTM thickness is lower than $50\ \mathrm{nm}$ due to the metal diffusion through HTM into perovskite, which could cause perovskite decomposition and increase charge recombination losses. Thicknesses above $70~\mathrm{nm}$ caused a notable reduction of PCE (e.g., $8\%$ for $130\mathrm{nm}$ ). The PCE evolution agrees well with the change of the fill factor (FF) and series resistance (Figure S2 and Table S1, Supporting Information), which reflects thicker undoped spiro-OMeTAD induce higher electrical resistance in devices. Overall, the devices with TE displayed large PCE variation for all thicknesses, indicating poor film reproducibility from TE, and possibly resulting from the uneven vapor particle distribution in the vacuum chamber. These results further reveal that the poor film quality from conventional TE is likely the major limiting factor for their poor performance in PSCs.
In spite of much smaller HTM thickness $(<130\ \mathrm{nm})$ ) from TE compared to that from SP process for spiro-OMeTAD $(>250\mathrm{nm})$ , TE based devices exhibited inferior PCEs and a wider efficiency distribution than those with SP undoped spiro-OMeTAD (Figure  1C). Considering the twisted spiro structure and high molecular energy in vacuum deposition, we infer that the disordered molecular arrangement or low stacking density in TE films could be the limiting factors. To verify this hypothesis, SATE was introduced to fabricate spiro-OMeTAD films by transferring the TE films into a solvent vapor atmosphere, followed by thermal annealing at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 5 min. A reactive solvent chlorobenzene was employed in SATE in order to induce partial dissolution and molecular rearrangement of spiro-OMeTAD. To probe this, variable-angle spectroscopic ellipsometry (VASE) was applied to probe the optical properties of SATE films, which enables film thicknesses measurements, as illustrated and summarized in Figure S3 (Supporting Information) and Figure 2A. About a $21\%$ decrease of thickness from 70 to $55~\mathrm{nm}$ was observed after placing the TE films in solvent vapor for $3{\mathrm{-}}7{\mathrm{~min}}$ (coded as SATE-3, SATE-5, and SATE-7, respectively). The film thickness reduction rate in SATE is approximately $3\mathrm{nm}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ , suggesting that the solvent annealing in SATE is a sufficient and reproducible way to modulate the molecular configuration and packing density. Notably, SATE-7 exhibited a large variation in film thickness ranging from 45 to $60\ \mathrm{nm}$ , implying that excessive posttreatment may cause deleterious effects on the film morphology or microstructure, which will be discussed later.
By dissolving the spiro-OMeTAD film in chlorobenzene and then measuring its absorption with ultraviolet–visible (UV–vis) spectroscopy, we quantified the SATE film density. The absorbance at $370\mathrm{nm}$ $\left(\varepsilon=7.53\times10^{4}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\mathrm{M}^{-1}\right.$ for spiro-OMeTAD) was used as a monitor for density estimation (Figure S4, Supporting Information).[33] Compared to TE film $(\bar{3}.60\ \times\ 10^{2\bar{3}}\ \mathrm{cm^{-3}})$ more than $25\%$ higher molecular density was reached for SATE-5 $(4.4\times10^{23}~\mathrm{cm}^{-3})$ and SATE-7 $(4.73\times10^{23}~\mathrm{cm}^{-3})$ ), which is comparable to the density of SP film (Table S2, Supporting Information). The large distribution in film density for SATE-7 mostly originates from thickness variation (Figure 2A). Besides, a decreased UV–vis absorption for SATE films with extended treatment time (Figure S6, Supporting Information) further verifies the reduction of film thickness, illustrating that SATE represents an efficient way to adjust molecular density of TE films.[34] The increase in film density with SATE is likely associated with the film microscopic structure, which was probed by AFM (Figure  2C). The bare perovskite film has an average root-mean-square (RMS) value of $34.55\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The surface RMS slightly increased to $39.6\ \mathrm{nm}$ for the film covered by spiroOMeTAD, which reflected that the disordered and loosely packed molecules in the TE film follow the roughness and surface morphology of perovskite. However, this low molecular stacking density and high surface roughness are undesirable for efficient charge collection in PSCs, which in part explains the low PCEs for TE devices (Figure  1C). In contrast, the average RMS values were greatly decreased to 28.8, 28.2, and $14.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ for SATE-3, SATE-5, and SATE-7, respectively. The smaller surface roughness indicates SATE contributes to the formation of condensed thin film. For comparison, spin-coated spiro-OMeTAD film displayed a uniform and smooth surface $\mathrm{^{\prime}R M S}<10~\mathrm{nm}$ ), which is reasonable considering its high thickness of $250~\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S5, Supporting Information) and solution-based deposition. These results indicate that SATE technique can efficiently modify the film topography, which could subsequently enhances the electronic interaction at interfaces and facilitate carrier transfer.[35] Undoped spiro-OMeTAD can potentially create a hydrophobic surface to inhibit moisture ingress and volatile species egress. Contact angle (CA) measurements revealed about $13\%$ increase of CA was obtained for the undoped SATE films with respect to SP film (Figure  2D). The contact angle slightly increased from $95.0^{\circ}$ to above $96.2^{\circ}$ with the extension of post-treatment time, which likely benefit from the corresponding higher film density. The higher contact angles for SATE films guarantee a more water-repellent property, which are desirable for device stability.
HTM film morphology determines the interfacial contacts with top metal electrode and thus imposes considerable influences on carrier recombination and charge collection yield. Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) is employed to visualize their microscopic structures (Figure 3A–D). All TE and SATE films appeared high uniform and homogeneous without discernible pinholes or defects. However, the underlying perovskite film in the case of SATE-7 exhibited noticeable changes in crystal morphology by showing blurred grain boundaries and large pinholes (Figure  3D). This significant change in film morphology is consistent with the large thickness variation of SATE-7, as displayed in Figure 2A. The results indicate that excessive solvent post-treatment could induce perovskite recrystallization as well as change in morphology. To confirm this effect, we further investigated the influences of CB vapors on the bare perovskite films. As shown in SEM (Figure S7, Supporting Information) and AFM (Figure S8, Supporting
Information), longer CB treatment induced more whitedots features and blurred grain boundaries on the perovskite surface. And the surface roughness of perovskite films was decreased from 34.4 to $28.4\mathrm{nm}$ as the increase of CB treatment time from 0 to 7 min. These results clearly indicated that excess CB vapor treatment could induce perovskite degradation. We also performed X-ray diffraction (XRD) to evaluate the crystallinity of SATE films (Figure S9, Supporting Information). No detectable diffraction peaks were observed in any samples, indicating the molecules are disordered and amorphous in SATE films. Previous work has reported that the Raman characteristics of evaporated spiro-OMeTAD provide information on structural properties.[36] Therefore, Raman spectra of different SATE samples were measured and analyzed (Figure S10, Supporting
Information). Similar Raman features were recorded for all SATE films, SP undoped as well as SP ones, confirming that all samples have amorphous structures. Additionally, Fourier  transform infrared (FTIR)  spectra were measured to investigate the structural change of spiro-OMeTAD after SATE (Figure S11, Supporting Information), which exhibited very similar features to the ones with SP, indicating no structural deformation occurred for spiro-OMeTAD during SATE.
Charge conducting properties of HTMs are crucial for charge dynamics and solar cell performance. Conductive AFM (C-AFM) was performed to evaluate the spatial conductivity (Figure  3E–H). As the post-treatment time increased from 3 to $5\ \mathrm{min}$ , the current in the SATE films was remarkably enhanced compared to TE ones. This result is mainly ascribed to improved film conductivity, which mostly resulted from the higher molecular packing density and lower film thickness. In addition, high spatial uniformity of electrical current in the whole film further confirms the low roughness and homogeneity of the SATE films on perovskite. By comparison, the SP film shows higher current across the whole film, but with some dot-like nonconductive region, which may be from the aggregated additives or some pinhole defects. Film conductivity was further evaluated via measuring the sheet resistance of devices based on glass/HTM/Ag by four-point probe (Figure S12 and Table S3, Supporting Information). About $80\%$ reduction of film series resistance from $1.106\times10^{11}$ to $2.332\times10^{10}~\Omega$ was achieved for the SATE-5 compared to TE ones. This result is in good agreement with the above C-AFM spectra. The resistance reduction for SATE films is the result of denser molecular packing and lower thickness, as illustrated in Figure  2A. The increased conducting property is essential to mitigate the charge accumulation at interfaces and boost the charge collection efficiency, thereby raising photovoltaic parameters in SATE based devices. Kelvin Probe Force Microscopy (KPFM) was employed to examine the surface potentials of SATE samples (Figure S13, Supporting Information and Figure 3I). About 70 and $37\mathrm{mV}$ higher surface potential were gained for SATE-5 and SATE-3 samples compared to TE ones $(910~\mathrm{mV}_{\perp}$ . The enhanced surface potential can accelerate interfacial hole injection and thus lead to efficient charge collection probability as well as suppression of charge recombination.[37]
Based on the above results, we propose a working mechanism of SATE (Figure  3J). First, in contrast to traditional TE, SATE enhances molecular packing density, which leads to a reduction in hole hopping distance and film sheet resistance, as well as improvement of interfacial electronic contact. Second, a higher built-in potential at the perovskite/HTM interfaces in the optimal SATE devices reduces charge accumulation and surface recombination. Third, the absence of hygroscopic additives and high density in SATE based undoped spiro-OMeTAD film inhibits both the moisture permeation and metal diffusion into perovskite. From these findings, we conclude that SATE is highly effective and versatile at modulating the physicochemical properties of organic thin films.
The photovoltaic performance of SATE based devices was measured from PSCs fabricated with a configuration of $\mathrm{ITO}/$ $\mathrm{SnO}_{2I}$ /perovskite/spiro-OMeTAD/Ag. The efficiency histogram for TE and SATE devices are summarized in Figure 4A and Table S4 (Supporting Information). SATE promotes a substantial increase in the device performance compared to TE. Specifically, both PCE and FF increased in the case of SATE devices ( $18.52\pm1.99\%$ for SATE-5, $15.24\pm3.08$ for SATE-3) compared with the counterparts made by TE $(12.80\pm2.44\%)$ . Notably, a much narrower PCE distribution was obtained for SATE-5 compared to other conditions, indicating that the optimal solvent treatment $(5~\mathrm{\min})$ improves device reproducibility. However, SATE-7 reduced PCE and increased efficiency variation $(14.90\pm2.81\%)$ , which mostly originated from the solvent inducing perovskite morphology change, as observed in Figure  3C. The PCE enhancement for SATE-5 is mainly attri­ buted to the substantial increase of FF $(74.63\pm4.45\%)$ together with reduced series resistance $(96.12~\pm~27.99$ ohms) compared to TE (FF: $58.43~\pm~12.13\%$ and series resistance: $192.80~\pm~78.78$ ohms) (Figure  4B and  4C). These further demonstrate that SATE is conducive to enhance the hole conductivity as well as charge collection efficiency. Moreover, higher contrast $(1.044\pm0.016\:\mathrm{V})$ for SATE-5 than the reference TE $(1.013\pm0.033\mathrm{V})$ was achieved, as shown in Figure S14 (Supporting Information), which again support the critical roles of SATE in suppressing interfacial recombination by contributing a denser barrier isolation between perovskite and top metal electrode.[38] Overall, devices with the optimal SATE-5 exhibited comparable performance to the devices based on the SP spiroOMeTAD (Table S4, Supporting Information). Besides, the hysteresis index of SATE devices is much lower than SP devices (Figure S15, Supporting Information), which may originate from the eliminated ion migration and accumulation in dopantfree SATE HTMs. We also examined other solvents in SATE process, including chlorobenzene, toluene, and iso-propanol (IPA). Both toluene and CB are effective for the improvement of PCE, but a more substantial improvement in performance was achieved by CB under the same post-treatment time. In contrast to the other two reactive solvents, IPA barely affected the SATE devices performance (Figure S16 and Table S5, Supporting ­Information). Given spiro-OMeTAD’s low molecular solubility in IPA, we speculate that partial dissolution of the film is essential for molecular arrangements in SATE process.
The current–voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ characteristics for the champion devices with different conditions are displayed in Figure 4D. In contrast to the low performance for TE based devices ( $\mathrm{{PCE}=}$ $14.68\%$ , $J_{\mathrm{sc}}=24.04~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , $V_{\mathrm{oc}}=0.975\mathrm{V}_{\mathrm{:}}$ and $\mathrm{FF}=62.62\%$ , the device with SATE-5 reached the champion efficiency up to $20.02\%$ $(J_{\mathrm{sc}}=24.89\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , $V_{\mathrm{oc}}=1.034~\mathrm{V},$ and $\mathrm{FF}=77.75\%$ , which represents the highest reported PCE for n–i–p PSCs based on evaporated organic or inorganic HTMs, as illustrated in Figure 4I and Table S6 (Supporting Information). This demonstrates that SATE can optimize the microscopic structures of undoped charge transport materials and boost optoelectronic performance. Moreover, the higher extracted slope from $J{-}V$ curves at open circuit for SATE-5 and SATE-3 compared to the TE indicates that solvent annealing is crucial for enhancing charge extraction at perovskite/HTM interface, and thus increasing the FF and $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ (Figure S17, Supporting Information). Their similar slopes at short circuit condition imply that similar drift transport properties are presented in all samples when the internal field reaches its maximum. These results were further validated by the significantly reduced series resistance for SATE-5 devices compared to TE, suggesting an increased charge carrier transporting property.[39] Compared to the high FF $(81.78\%)$ for traditional SP spiro-OMeTAD (Table S4, Supporting
Information), further optimization and increase of the SATE film conductivity is required for additional improvements of efficiency. It is noted that light soaking is needed to obtain a stabilized efficiency (Figure S18, Supporting Information), as similarly observed in previous report.[40] We have further evaluated the external quantum efficiency (EQE) of the optimal devices based on SATE-5, as shown in Figure S19A (Supporting Information). The devices exhibited high EQE of over $85\%$ in the range of $400{-}800~\mathrm{nm}$ , resulting in an integrated current density of about $23.1\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , which is comparable with the initial $J_{s c}$ during $J{-}V$ measurement, but slightly lower than the maximum $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ recorded after light soaking, as shown in Figure S19B (Supporting Information). This is reasonable considering the low light intensity used in EQE measurement and the high light sensitivity of the device parameters. In order to examine the hypothesis that excess solvent annealing (SA) in SATE condition impacts on both spiro-OMeTAD layer and perovskite layer, SA was directly introduced to as-prepared perovskite layers, and followed by TE spiro-OMeTAD. As shown in Figure  S20 and Table  S7 (Supporting Information), the devices based on SA perovskite layers exhibited a significantly decreased PCE from $12.80\%$ to $5.62\%$ in SA time range of $3{\mathrm{-}}7{\mathrm{~min}}$ , suggesting that the SA time in SATE plays essential roles in optimizing the device performance. In order to examine the influences of SA process on the SP undoped samples, we fabricated devices based on different SA time on SP undoped films, namely SASP undoped-3, SASP undoped-5, and SASP undoped-7. Their photovoltaic performance was displayed in Figure S21 and Table S8 (Supporting Information). It is noted that all devices exhibited comparable photovoltaic parameters, indicating little impacts of SA on SP undoped devices, which is probably due to negligible thickness change in SP undoped film by SA method.
It is reported that the major potential drop (built-in electric field, $V_{\mathrm{bi}})$ exists at perovskite/spiro-OMeTAD interface in planar structure devices.[41] We performed Mott–Schottky analysis on various samples by examining the capacitance-voltage characteristics (Figure 4E, Note S1 and Figure S22, Supporting Information).[42] The higher $V_{\mathrm{bi}}$ for SATE-3 and SATE-5 than TE means solvent treatment facilitates charge separation and transport, as well as carrier collection. Meanwhile, the charge density at the perovskite/HTM interfaces in SATE-5 devices is found to be 0.83 times lower than those of the TE based devices, indicating hole accumulation was greatly reduced by more sufficient interfacial charge transfer. Electrical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) was applied to investigate the transport and recombination resistance at the perovskite/HTM interface (Figure 4F). The fitted parameters calculated from the equivalent circuit are listed in Table S9 (Supporting Information). Much higher attained $R_{\mathrm{rec}}$ values for SATE-3 and SATE-5 than that of TE devices (326.4 Ω, $532.8~\Omega$ , and $697.4\Omega$ for TE, SATE-3, and SATE-5, respectively) suggests that nonradiative recombination has been suppressed by solvent treatment, which is likely due to the faster charge transport and reduced charge accumulation at interfaces in SATE-5 devices.
It is reported that morphological stability of the HTM film is a critical parameter for influencing device reliability.[44,45] The morphology of SP, TE and SATE films after $2500\mathrm{~h~}$ storage in $30\%$ relative humidity (RH) was examined by SEM (Figure 5A–F). Smooth, uniform and pinhole-free films were maintained in both regions of Ag metal and spiro-OMeTAD in the case of SATE-5. In contrast, severe morphology changes were noted for spin-coated doped spiro-OMeTAD by showing large pinholes in the film (Figure  5D). These results suggest the absence of hygroscopic dopants in the SATE film is beneficial for inhibiting pinhole formation and mitigating the metal diffusion through HTM layer. High film density of HTM is critical for PSC thermal stability, since the compact film can prevent the metal (Ag) and volatile species $(\mathrm{I^{-}})$ from ingress and egress. AFM topography of SP, SATE-5, and TE samples after thermal treatment at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ were studied to monitor the evolution of film surface homogeneity (Figure 5G–J and Figure S27, Supporting Information). A large number of pinholes are formed in the SP HTM films, producing a large variation in film roughness. However, no visible change in film roughness was observed for both TE and SATE-5 samples. We also evaluated the microstructures of films based on SP, TE, and SATE-5 by SEM, as shown in Figure S28 (Supporting Information). In contrast to large number of holes generated in SP samples, the films for both TE and SATE-5 exhibited pinhole-free features after $^{1\mathrm{{h}}}$ annealing. But higher uniformity was obtained for SATE-5 samples than TE ones, further indicating the critical roles of SA process in improving the spiro-OMeTAD film quality, as explained in Figure 3J.
Environmental stability of SATE and SP devices was compared in air condition with $30\%$ RH and at room temperature (Figure 6A). A clear metal diffusion and perovskite deformation was observed for SP devices after $1000\mathrm{~h~}$ . However, no visible change in SATE film was detected. When the unsealed devices were stored in a $70\%$ RH for $100\mathrm{{h}}$ (Figure 6B), a complete damage of the active layers occurred for SP devices, indicating the additives in HTMs acts as accelerator to induce device degradation. The devices stability was also examined in maximum power point (MPP) under ambient condition $(40\%$ RH), as shown in Figure  6C. A significant degradation with around $40\%$ losses of initial PCEs was observed in SP devices after 300s. In contrast, $590\%$ of initial performance was kept for SATE devices. We further explored the long-term stability of devices in air condition with $30\%$ RH for $2500\mathrm{h}$ . Consequently, the devices with SP spiro-OMeTAD lost $50\%$ of initial performance after $650\mathrm{h}$ (Figure 6D) and subsequently no reliable $J{-}V$ curves can be detected due to complete degradation of devices. In contrast, PSCs based on SATE exhibited outstanding stability by maintained more than $85\%$ of initial PCE after $2500\mathrm{~h~}$ and out-performed, TE devices with around $70\%$ of initial PCE. In addition, the devices based on SATE exhibited remarkable stability in high humidity condition $(60-70\%$ RH) by maintaining more than $70\%$ of initial PCE after $^{120\mathrm{~h~}}$ (Figure S29, Supporting Information). In contrast, the SP devices maintained less than $60\%$ of these initial value after $^{72\mathrm{~h~}}$ . Additive-free SATE spiro-OMeTAD films with low defects and high density sufficiently block water intake and metal diffusion, contributing to remarkable enhancement of device stability.
## 3. Conclusion
In summary, we have developed a versatile SATE approach for depositing high quality and stable undoped spiro-OMeTAD films. Compared to conventional TE method, SATE enables effective modulation of organic film’s microstructures, including surface morphology, film density and uniformity. SATE is a tunable process that can optimize film properties, such as electrical conductivity, hydrophobicity, barrier compactness, and interfacial stability. Consequently, fine optimization of SATE creates a uniform, dense, less resistive and stable spiroOMeTAD film. As a result, the devices attain a $36\%$ enhancement of PCE from $14.68\%$ (TE) to $20.02\%$ (SATE), which is the highest reported PCE for evaporated HTMs in n–i–p PSCs. Moreover, SATE based devices demonstrate impressive environmental and thermal stability. More than $85\%$ of initial performance was maintained for SATE devices after $2500\mathrm{~h~}$ in air condition with $30\%$ humidity. The high efficiency with simultaneously improved stability reveals the vital importance of molecular configuration to device performance, and these demonstrate the potential of SATE for enhancing the reliability and scalability of PSCs.
